<https://w3id.org/framester/data/framestercore/ExperiencerObj> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://github.com/corrado877/CognitiveBiasOntologies/blob/main/Clustering_illusion> <https://github.com/corrado877/CognitiveBiasOntologies/blob/main/Clustering_illusion#user_story> "Sarah, a regular lottery who enjoys testing her luck with weekly ticket purchases. Over the past month, Sarah notices a curious pattern emerging in the winning numbers of the local lottery. She observes that the winning numbers often consist of consecutive digits, such as 12, 13, 14, or alternating pairs like 5 and 7.\nExcited by this apparent pattern, Sarah starts to develop a theory that the lottery draws are not entirely random. She begins meticulously analysing past winning numbers, searching for more evidence to support her hypothesis. With each new draw, Sarah eagerly checks the results, hoping to uncover additional patterns or trends.\nAs the weeks pass, Sarah becomes increasingly convinced that she has cracked the code to winning the lottery. She starts sharing her findings with friends and family, boasting about her newfound strategy for picking winning numbers. Some of her acquaintances start to believe in Sarah's theory, intrigued by the apparent predictability of the lottery draws.\nSarah decides to invest more money into buying lottery tickets, confident that her strategy will lead to big wins. However, despite her efforts, Sarah's luck doesn't seem to improve. She continues to match only a few numbers here and there, never coming close to hitting the jackpot." .
<https://w3id.org/framester/data/framestercore/ExperiencerObj> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#disjointWith> <https://w3id.org/framester/data/framestersyn/Person> .
<https://w3id.org/framester/data/framestercore/ExperiencerObj> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Some phenomenon (the Stimulus) provokes a particular emotion in an Experiencer. Nightmare on Elm Street scared me silly." .
